From: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] adaptive: price the unpriced part of a step in the budget objective

The budget objective divides expected accepted tokens by the profiled draft and
verify costs only:

    draft_budget = argmax estimated_accepted / (draft_cost_ms + verify_cost_ms)

Those curves come from replaying captured graphs over dummy batches, so they cover
exactly two phases of a step. Everything else - pipeline wait, sampling, state
updates, connector work - is absent from the denominator. Expected accepted tokens
are concave in the budget while the priced cost keeps rising, so a denominator
missing its constant term peaks too early: the objective systematically under-
verifies, worst where the unpriced share is largest.

Measured on PP6 with the step tracer, unpriced = step period - priced forward:

    one request per step   period 43.4 ms, forward  5.0 ms -> 38.4 ms unpriced (88%)
    six requests per step  period 18.1 ms, forward 11.3 ms ->  6.8 ms unpriced (38%)

and the traced consequence matched the bias exactly: at c1 the manager trimmed 69%
of steps to 4 of 6 rows and prose c1 came out 7.0% below the feature-off baseline,
while the step period barely moved (42.6 vs 41.1 ms) - work saved that never
materialised as time.

Add the missing term, estimated online rather than assumed. The gap between
consecutive budget decisions is one step of wall time on the deciding rank;
subtracting the priced cost of the step that just ran leaves what no curve
accounted for. It is kept in a per-request-count ring and used as a median,
because the quantity is really a property of pipeline saturation, not of row
count: it fell from 38.4 ms to 6.8 ms as rows grew, so a rows-linear term - the
form a "fixed plus per-row" decomposition would suggest - has the wrong sign
here and is not identifiable anyway (inside a bucket the row count barely moves).
Fewer than 8 samples, an out-of-band gap, or a negative residual all leave the
objective exactly as upstream wrote it, and VLLM_ADAPTIVE_VERIFICATION_PRICED_FIXED_COST=0
turns the change off wholesale.

Decision locality is unchanged: only the rank that computes the budget observes or
uses this, so the PP relay keeps every other rank on the shape it published.

Simulated at the measured cost levels, with representative prose-like survival
probabilities: without the term the optimum is 3 of 5 drafts (the observed 4-row
trim at c1); with 38.4 ms it is the full 5. At six requests the shift is small
(6 to 12 of 30 drafts), i.e. trimming behaviour at high concurrency is largely
preserved, which is the intent - the term is only as loud as the unpriced cost is.

Signed-off-by: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
